\f1\fs22 Tony landed in much the same way a sack of overripe melons might. The momentum of his fall threw his head back, which collided with the suburban\'92s back window. He winced and rubbed the flowering bruise under his hair, and thus had no mind for who he was sharing the back seat with.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hello Tony,\'92 Alexander said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony blinked the pain from his eyes until his vision aligned with the sound of the familiar voice. \'91Koalemos? What, the tax? Why\'97?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m here to deliver you from the pickle jar. Or throw you in; I haven\'92t decided yet.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony backed against the suburban door, his pelvis grinding against the wheel arch. The synapses of his aching brain blew to pollen as he tried to process why Alexander would be threatening him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander released a long impatient breath as he drew his index-thin knees up and planted his feet on the floor. \'91I had a whole day of production meetings and package editing today\'97I have some truly reprehensible Cloudborne boofheads for this week\'92s 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 . Yet, here I am again, running investigative errands for the Commissioner, only this time, I find you\'92re at the heart of them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What are you talking about?\'92 Tony shunted his panicked thoughts up a gear, and achieved the break-necked speeds of a cement wagon with a bustle axle. \'91Look, if it\'92s about that suit I nearly ran over a few weeks\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander dismissed Tony\'92s fumbled words with a wave of a hand and a knot in his eyebrows. \'91Don\'92t make it worse for yourself, my dear friend. We already have quite enough to lock you in a cell, and throw away the whole prison.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A look of horror salted Tony\'92s face. \'91What are\'97who\'97?\'92 he stammered until Alexander\'92s narrowing eyes and thinning lips drained him of bluster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91All right,\'92 Alexander said with some resignation and haughty dismissal. \'91Polish it that way, if you must.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Why you?\'92 Tony pleaded. \'91Why does it have to be you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Why me, ever, Tony? The same reason as always: they want my rubber stamp on a fix-up that no one will ever look beyond.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Everyone is afraid of your mother.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Quite so, and it\'92s an unceasing curse, I assure you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A curse?\'92 Tony demanded, and almost launched on a litany of ways Alexander has benefitted from privilege. The effort cramped his chest, and it felt almost as if his small intestine had slithered up through his neck and tied his tongue in a bow. Alexander had it easy, Tony bitterly ruminated: born in a comfortable Airborne home to a socially lofty father and an increasingly business-adept mother, the now terrifying Eloise Shakti, at least by reputation; afforded the best private schooling, best tutors, and ultimately futile learning enhancement tech; his stage-managed acceptance into the Roger Caleb Rogerson Police Academy, a career path sneered at by most Airborne, but which Eloise managed to paint as a prestigious career move; his admission into said academy with no more qualifications than a heavily redacted medical report and psychiatric assessment, but from which the line, 
\f2\i Monkeys retain information better
\f1\i0 , was leaked by a since missing journalist; his graduation despite failing every academic requirement and his inability to even shoot straight with a \'91bag and tag\'92 Particle Pulse Pistol; and his fortuitously timed assignment to the Public Affairs Bureau, as Police Video Streaming and Public Safety Broadcasting were on the rise.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s only innate qualities were his ability to smile, to talk to camera, and to capitulate and accept fawning offers to lighten his load whenever his load exceeded the first straw on a camel\'92s back. As the son of Eloise Shakti, Alexander\'92s social status exceeded everyone\'92s in the Force, including the Commissioner.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony tried not to be bitter about it. After all, being friend and cleaner for the most bumbling independently wealthy clod in existence was a good career move.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just be thankful,\'92 Alexander injected into the uncomfortable pause Tony left while he ground his back teeth to dust, \'91they sent me to round you up, and not any of those PAPER agents you beat up in that seedy Memphis Mafia Bar.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Tony uttered with unctuous incredulity. \'91They\'92re the ones\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No use arguing with me about it, my recidivating pal. I doubt any PAPER agent would claim they were bettered by a cleaner, unless it was true. I\'92m not the one here laying the charges\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What else have they got on me?\'92 Tony dared ask while his eyes moistened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Let\'92s see \'85 stealing some highly-classified gadget: a commercial-in-confidence fridge magnet, I believe they said. A disc of some kind?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A Gyges Disc?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, so you admit it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No!\'92 Tony\'92s heart sank and his spleen filled with dread as he remembered he\'92d left Sarah alone in the room with the Gyges Disc. His heightened state of alarm conjured an image of the dreadful thing, salivating and sticky, slinking its way over the sticky carpet, rappelling the quilt cover to adhere itself once again to Sarah\'92s perfect, oh-so-perfect, body.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ll also be charged with conspiracy. Those sage accomplices of yours\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Accomplices? I never met them before last night.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And you can prove this?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How about you do some basic police work, and go ask them?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I would, but they\'92re quite seedy characters, these wizard-goons you\'92re in league with. Theirs is a seedy business. I\'92m sure you\'92re well aware, they have offices on the Travelator. I would pay them a visit, I assure you, but their carriages aren\'92t due back in the region for another three or four weeks, barring any trans-dimensional storms.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony shook his head and blinked, as the suburban turned a hard right. All these accusations were overwhelming.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The there\'92s Travis Teh\'92s murder \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The shotgun.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You said it was suicide!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It was suicide, according to official investigations. But we know better, right?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I never would have been there if you hadn\'92t requested I come clean up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Exactly. Very clever of you Tony.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Clever?\'92 Tony felt like a guppy in a net, losing its mind with terror.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, all of these suspicious incidents have one thing in common, Tony: your presence. You\'92re always at the scene of the crime.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s all stupid coincidences. Please, Alex, see reason.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Reason is the last gambit of a scoundrel, Tony. What about the PAPER agent who was found murdered in her apartment? Stabbed to death, like a pig, with a weapon we believe is some kind of triangular ice-pick. You really have it in for PAPER agents, it seems.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I have no idea who you are talking about.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91She was at the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0  the night you blew it up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I can\'92t have murdered her. You saw me lying injured on the road after the blast.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, so you don\'92t deny blowing up the 
\f2\i Inn
\f1\i0 . Got you there, my chum.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I do deny it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well you didn\'92t immediately deny it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s because I was busy denying the murder.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What murder?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The girl with an ice pick in her.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How do you know she was murdered with an ice pick?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You just told me!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Why are you getting so defensive about this murder accusation? Is it because you are guilty?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander. How could I have killed her? You rescued me after the blast.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I have some memory of that,\'92 Alexander conceded. \'91But since then, I checked the security cams from the hover cruiser, and there\'92s no evidence of us picking you up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But you did pick me up. Me and Sarah.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s nostrils flared at the mention of Sarah, and he appeared to recede into glassy-eyed reverie, as if recalling a pleasant dream.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander! Alex!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander came back to the suburban.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Surely you remember kicking me out of the copt onto the roof of that hospital?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what was the name of the hospital?\'92 Alexander pounced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony hesitated, realising he couldn\'92t recall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Was it St Gertha\'92s of the Sacred Goitre? Our Lady of Perpetual Infection?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Um, the Gertha one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ha! It was Our Lady of the Sacred Goitre.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So you did roll me out of the cruiser.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And you lied about it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You lied about the name, which makes me think, maybe neither of us was ever there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Have you gone mad?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91If I have, it\'92s because someone has implanted false memories of me rescuing you\'97I mean, really, does that sound like something I would do?\'97to cover up your role in all these crimes?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who the tax would implant false memories in you, if such a thing can even be done?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sure it was those sages, since they have also disappeared.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I thought you said they were on the Travelator, and their carriages won\'92t cycle back around for another three or four weeks.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Exactly! You can\'92t get much more suspicious than that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony crumpled, and hung his head, the only tense part of his body was his balled fists. \'91I\'92m totally taxed, aren\'92t I?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander placed a soft hand on Tony\'92s thigh, which felt more over-familiar than sympathetic. \'91Look,\'92 Alexander said. \'91I like you, Tony. For a grainy, Layer-soaked Cloudborne, you\'92re OK. And you\'92ve certainly got me out of some professional jams in the past.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Don\'92t I know it,\'92 Tony said bitterly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I swear, there were some monumental stains I thought you\'92d never get out. And the stenches! Oh, my current accounts! Some of those would dissolve a plate of braised pus. Including the plate.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So why are you arresting me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Arresting you?\'92 Alexander was genuinely shocked by Tony assumption. \'91Tony, I\'92m only trying to ascertain how much trouble you are in, and given your confessions here today\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But I haven\'92t confessed to anything!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander chortled and squeezed Tony\'92s thigh even more inappropriately. \'91That\'92s good. You keep that up and you might only get five or six decades for this mess.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Five or six\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, I know, it sounds draconian, but you Cloudborne need the heel to behave. You know that, Tony. But look, I really want to help you, and I\'92m sure you understand I could help you. I have the power, the influence.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You mean, you have the mother.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander retracted his hand as if bitten by a snake. \'91That\'92s unbecoming, Tony, especially since I\'92m genuinely trying to help you.\'92 Alexander left a long pause, in which Tony became acutely aware he was about to be propositioned. \'91But look, there\'92s also a thing you could do for me,\'92 went on with soothing tones. \'91A truly life-affirming, good thing, Tony, perhaps the most beautiful thing you\'92ve ever done in your miserable sunless life.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony swore under his breath and decided to say nothing. Perhaps six decades in prison was the better option.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You see,\'92 Alexander said, and Tony\'92s alarm escalated, \'91I\'92ve fallen in love.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the tax?\'92 Tony said with some relief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I know, right? She\'92s the most beautiful, most angelic woman I\'92ve ever met. She\'92s literally perfect, Tony. Per-fect.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, um \'85 that\'92s nice, Alex. I\'92m \'85 happy for you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander became solemn, and limply clasped Tony\'92s hands in his own. \'91Tony, please. Tell me where you\'92re keeping Sarah Jacobson.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony chilled to the marrow. There was no scenario in which he could give Sarah up to Alexander. Even if his intentions were neurotically pure, Sarah\'92s association with Agent Jones, the Sages, and the Gyges Disc were not so very different from his own. She could be in as much trouble as he was, even if Alexander couldn\'92t see it. Tax! Alexander may want to apprehend her too. \'91Um, who?\'92 Tony asked without conviction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Come now, Tony. You know Sarah. She was lying next to you, unconscious on the street, after the bombing. She might have died if I hadn\'92t rescued her.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ha! So you admit you rescued us from the bombing! That means we\'92re innocent. Who would stick around for their own bomb to go off?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It means Sarah is innocent. You, I\'92m not so sure about. What I am sure about is, Sarah and I were meant to meet. Even if those memories are fake, we would have found each other any way. I just know it. We are meant to be together. And I\'92ve done everything since to make that happen, which means, of course, we will inevitably be together. Now, which side of my considerable influence would you like to be on?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony was in two minds as to which would be worse: being liked as co-conspirator in all these allegations, or being Alexander\'92s love interest. \'91I\'92m sorry, Alex. I\'92d tell you where she is, if I had any idea.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s demeanour hardened. \'91I\'92m a nice guy, Tony. A good catch. And rich, of course, not that it should matter, but it does. And I\'92m a excellent lover, Tony. Excellent. Skilled with all those lady parts. I swear I can locate a woman\'92s cervix in less than thirty seconds, even in the dark. Women love that stuff. It\'92s inconceivable she would not have thanked me for all the flowers, and muffins and vibrators I sent her while she was recovering from the bomb explosion. I can only conclude that her disappearance is due to kidnapping, a kidnapping which I\'92m now certain you perpetrated.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony knew he was taxed for sure, caught between being fit up for a series of crimes, and Alexander\'92s obsession for a woman who probably hasn\'92t given him a moment\'92s fond thought. \'91I don\'92t know where she is,\'92 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tony, you must help me. I must find Sarah at all costs, even if it costs me my exceedingly valuable bachelorhood.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sorry.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander stopped fondling Tony\'92s thigh. He banged the roof of the suburban, and the driver pulled to the kerb.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s happening?\'92 Tony asked, his alarm escalating.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander opened the door and stepped out. \'91No cause for alarm, Tony. I know who you are.\'92 Alexander winked and touched his nose. \'91I have every confidence you\'92ll find your way out of this.\'92 He shut the door, tapped the suburban roof, and watched the vehicle screech away.
